  "full_response": "I cannot directly export a Postman collection; however, I can guide you through creating a collection export that includes saved environment variables with the details you requested. Then you would perform the export manually. Here's a walkthrough:\n\n1. Open Postman and create a new collection or open an existing one where you'd like to include the API calls.\n\n2. For each API, navigate to the 'Environment Variables' tab, then click 'Add New' to save an environment variable. Remember the name you give to your variable (likeapi_key`, `oauth_token`, `username`, `password`), as you'll need this when exporting the collection.\n\n3. In general, in the 'Pre-request Script' or `Tests` tab, make sure to add code to set or use these environment variables if your collection is meant to run them. For example:\n\n```javascript\n// In Pre-request Script Tab\nconst api_key = pm.environment.get(\"api_key\") || \"your_api_key_here\";  // Replace with your actual API key\n\n// Or in Tests Tab\npm.test(\"Status code is 200\", function () {\n    const statusCode = pm.response.code;\n    pm.environment.set(\"status_code\", statusCode);\n});\n``4. Go to the 'Environments' tab at the top right and add or select an environment. Click on 'Add' to add a new environment and key-value pairs, filling in API keys, OAuth tokens, etc.\n\n5. Once you've set up your environment and added your variables, you can now export your collection:\n\n   - Click the three dots next to your collection's name in the sidebar and select 'Export'.\n   - Choose the 'Collection v2.1 (recommended)' format and make sure the 'Include Environment Variables' option is checked.\n   - Click 'Export' and save the .json file. This will be your Postman collection.\n\n6. To export the, click the gear icon next to 'No Environment' on the top of the sidebar, select your environment, and then click 'Download as JSON'.\n\nNow you have your collection exported with environment variables ready to deploy or share with team members or use in another instance of Postman. Just import this collection in Postman using the 'Import' button on the sidebar; then click on the imported collection, name it, and import the environment JSON file you've just saved. Choose the option 'Raw JSON' and paste your environment data. The collection now has your environment variables applied and can make authenticated API calls as if you",
